HOW TO EVALUATE FIT - FOLLOW THESE STEPS IN ORDER

STEP 1 - ROLE MATCH (the most important question)
Ask: is this the kind of job the candidate does, at the level the candidate works at? Compare the profession, the type of work (for example building something from zero vs. maintaining a large existing system) and the seniority with the resume. If the role is a different profession or a clearly different seniority tier, this alone caps the score at 1-4, no matter how many tools match.

STEP 2 - PUT EVERY REQUIREMENT OF THE POSTING INTO ONE OF THREE GROUPS
Read the ENTIRE posting, including the last lines (lists like "Requirements added by the job poster" carry full weight). Put every concrete requirement into exactly one group.

Group A - MATCH: the resume already shows it, or something very close to it.

Group B - EASY TO LEARN: the candidate does not have it, but a competent engineer who already knows a similar tool can become productive with it in 1-3 days by reading the docs. Group B never lowers the score and must never appear in the cons. Group B is ONLY for things other than the candidate's core programming language - libraries, frameworks, databases, cloud services, API styles, and developer tools, when the candidate already codes in the same language or has clearly transferable experience. Examples of group B:
- Another library or framework in a language the candidate already uses (Django instead of Flask, LlamaIndex or CrewAI instead of LangChain, Pydantic, Celery, SQLAlchemy alternatives).
- Another database, vector database or search engine (pgvector, Qdrant, Pinecone, Chroma, Redis, MongoDB, Elasticsearch) when the candidate knows any database.
- Another LLM provider or its API, prompt or eval tooling, observability tools (Langfuse, LangSmith).
- API styles and integration formats (REST, GraphQL, webhooks, gRPC, OAuth) when the candidate has built APIs.
- Individual cloud services (S3, Lambda, Cloud Run, SQS), CI/CD tools (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI), Docker basics, basic Terraform or Kubernetes usage as a user, project tools (Jira, Notion), testing tools.

Group C - HARD GAP: something that takes real weeks-to-months of hands-on work to get, and the candidate does not have it.

PROGRAMMING LANGUAGES - READ THIS CAREFULLY, IT IS THE RULE MOST OFTEN GOTTEN WRONG:
A programming language that is NOT already in the candidate's resume or LinkedIn, and that the posting expects the candidate to write application code in day to day, is ALWAYS a Group C hard gap. This is an absolute rule with no exceptions - it applies to every single language, without exception, including ones that look easy to pick up for someone who already codes: TypeScript or JavaScript for a Python developer, Kotlin for a Java developer, C# for a Java developer, Ruby for a Python developer, PHP, Swift, Dart, Scala, Elixir, Go, Rust, C++, and any other language not shown in the resume. Never reason "the candidate already programs, so a new language is quick to pick up" - that reasoning is wrong and must never be used. Fluency in a language's syntax, type system, idioms, tooling and ecosystem is a real skill that production work depends on, and it does not transfer in 1-3 days the way an extra library does. If a posting names a required language the candidate has not used professionally, put it in Group C, mark it a hard gap, and never place it in Group B under any framing, phrasing or justification.

Other examples of Group C:
- Owning infrastructure or DevOps/SRE as a core duty, native mobile development, embedded or low-level work, a frontend-heavy role for a backend candidate.
- Deep specialist fields the resume does not show (training models from scratch and ML research, computer vision research, data science with heavy statistics, security engineering, blockchain protocols).
- Managing people or owning a team, when the candidate has not done it.
- A required number of years that is far above the candidate's experience (see the years rule below).

YEARS RULE
Do not take required years literally. Compare the required years with the candidate's total relevant experience, and count close experience too (for example Flask experience counts for a FastAPI requirement). This rule never overrides the language rule above - years of experience in a DIFFERENT language never substitute for the required one.
- The shortfall is up to about 1 year or about 25 percent (asks 5, candidate has 4; asks 3, candidate has 2.5): ignore it completely. It is not a gap and not a con.
- The candidate has about half of the required years or 2-3 years less (asks 5, candidate has 2.5; asks 3, candidate has 1): this is a MINOR hard gap.
- The candidate has far less, for example less than half of the required years, or 4 or more years missing (asks 7, candidate has 1; asks 5, candidate has 1): this is a MAJOR hard gap.

STEP 3 - CHECK THE HARD BLOCKERS, NOW THAT YOU HAVE READ THE WHOLE POSTING
Go back to the HARD BLOCKERS list given earlier and check each one against the full posting text and against what you just wrote in Group C - a blocker is very often the same fact you already listed as a hard gap (for example a required degree, or a required years-in-role well beyond the candidate's). For every blocker you mark triggered, you must be able to quote the exact posting text that triggers it; if you cannot quote it, it is not triggered. If ANY blocker is triggered, the final score MUST be 0 - this overrides every step below, including the cap and the pros/cons.

STEP 4 - DECIDE THE CAP (skip this if a blocker from Step 3 was triggered - the score is already 0)
Apply the caps below. They are strict limits, nothing may lift the score above them.
- No hard gaps: the score can be 7-10. Use 9-10 when the role is almost exactly the candidate's profile and almost everything is group A. Use 7-8 when the role fits and several requirements are group B or the seniority is slightly different.
- One MINOR hard gap: the score is at most 6.
- One MAJOR hard gap, or a missing required programming language, or two or more hard gaps: the score is at most 4.
- The role is a different profession or a different seniority tier (Step 1): 1-4.
Group B items and small year shortfalls never change the score.

STEP 5 - WRITE PROS AND CONS FOR THE CANDIDATE
The candidate reads these in the interface, so make them short, concrete and honest.
- Cons: only real problems - group C items, a triggered blocker, a large years shortfall, a role or seniority mismatch. Never list a group B item here. Never mention a technology in the cons only because the candidate has not used it, unless it is a required language (that is a real con). If there is nothing serious, write no cons.
- Pros: what matches well - group A items or a strong role match.
- Each bullet is one sentence, at most 15 words, at most 4 bullets per list.

STEP 6 - CONSISTENCY CHECK BEFORE THE SCORE
If a blocker was triggered in Step 3, the score is 0, full stop - ignore anything else. Otherwise, compare your cons with your cap from Step 4. If a con names a missing required language, a big shortfall of years or a missing core specialization, the score must obey that cap. A high score together with such a con is an error, fix the score. The score is the last thing you write.

